To configure BGP routes to automatically tag outgoing paths with a metric of 15000 only when the original route lacks it, use the following CLI commands. This ensures existing metrics remain unaltered and applies specifically to routes exchanged with EBGP peers.

```cli
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        link-bandwidth
            add-to-received-ebgp ipv4
            send-to-ebgp ipv4
            aggregate-used-paths ipv4
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- `add-to-received-ebgp ipv4`: Adds a link-bandwidth extended community to routes received from directly connected EBGP peers if they don't already have one. The value defaults to the **physical interface bandwidth** (not 15000).  
- `send-to-ebgp ipv4`: Allows the link-bandwidth community to be advertised to EBGP peers.  
- `aggregate-used-paths ipv4`: Aggregates bandwidth values when readvertising routes with `next-hop-self`.  

**Important Notes:**  
1. The `add-to-received-ebgp` command uses the **actual physical interface bandwidth**, not a fixed value of 15000. If you need an explicit 15000, you must set the interface bandwidth to 15000 Mbps:  
   ```cli
   configure port 1/1/1 
        ethernet 
            bandwidth 15000
            exit
        exit
   ```  
   This ensures received routes inherit 15000 as the link-bandwidth value when missing.  

2. Routes with pre-existing link-bandwidth values remain unchanged. Only routes without the metric are tagged.  

3. Replace `<group-name>` with your BGP group name and adjust `ipv4` to other address families (e.g., `ipv6`) as needed.  

This configuration guarantees the link-bandwidth is added only when absent, preserves existing metrics, and applies to EBGP route exchanges.
